    "created_at": "2025-09-16T11:17:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-16T11:17:08Z",
    "body": "> I agree with [@noamr](https://github.com/noamr) that it should always skip/avoid the transition.\n\nBut that would force the author to maintain two different ways of thinking about this:\n\n- Same-Document: detect `hasUAVisualTransition` on `PopStateEvent` in order to skip own transition.\n- Cross-Document with Navigation API interception: detect `hasUAVisualTransition` on `NavigatEvent` in order to skip.\n- Cross-Document: it’s already skipped _(… unless you’ve opted in)_\n\n>  Places where both are acceptable seems like the edge case, and in those cases we can add a declarative opt in … and in that case we'd need `hasUAVisualTransition` somewhere in JS, or as a CSS pseudo class.\n\nSo we agree on adding `hasUAVisualTransition` to `NavigationActivation` either way, regardless of whether we decide to auto-skip the cross-doc VT or not.\n\nIf we do skip by default, my fear is that authors won’t include a `hasUAVisualTransition` check for cross-doc navigations and get used to that. And when they later do opt-in after all, then they suddenly need to add the check after all … so why not make it so that they always need to have the check in place?\n\nI’d rather give authors control on _disallowing_ the ua transition themselves:\n\n```css\n@view-transition {\n  navigation: auto;\n  ua-transition: none;\n}\n```\n\n_(The initial value would be `normal` which is “do the UA transition”)_\n\nThis would also open up the path to later on allow them to wire a custom VT to the swipe-to-go-back gesture.\n\n```css\n@view-transition {\n  navigation: auto;\n  ua-transition: my-custom-one;\n}\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
